<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lebanon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lebanon is a mountainous country on the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast, historically famous for its cedar forests. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> drew on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> of Lebanon for its building projects. Shia scholars from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-amil">Jabal Amil</a> in southern Lebanon were invited to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>, where they helped establish Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as the state religion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lebanon.jpg" alt="Lebanon" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henri Pharaon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Lebanon.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Holy See of Cilicia survives at Antelias in <b>Lebanon</b>, and the lion, emblem of the Cilician state, stands on the coat of arms of Armenia today.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>At its peak the Neo-Assyrian economy channelled the agricultural surpluses of Mesopotamia, the timber of <b>Lebanon</b>, the metals of Anatolia, the horses of the Zagros and the luxury goods of Phoenician trade into the construction of enormous royal cities at Nimrud, Khorsabad and Nineveh.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Byblos, called Gubla by its own people and in cuneiform texts, Gebal in the Hebrew Bible and Jbeil today, lies on a low headland on the coast of <b>Lebanon</b> about 35 kilometres north of Beirut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He defeated the Hasmonean Jews, and at its zenith his realm stretched over parts of the Caucasus and what is now south-eastern Turkey, Iran, Syria and <b>Lebanon</b>, one of the most powerful states of the Roman East.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-armenia-artaxiad">Kingdom of Armenia (Artaxiad) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Lebanon</b> has held no census since 1932, when Christians were 53% of the population: the relative size of its religious communities is too sensitive for its confessional politics to count.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lebanese-republic">Lebanese Republic · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Malayan and Malaysian troops have served in United Nations missions from the Congo in the 1960s to Somalia and Bosnia in the 1990s and <b>Lebanon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
